{"name":"Loire Valley","hex":"#577042","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"valley","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elongated depression cast between hills or mountains, often with a river flowing through it.","An area which drains itself into a river."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form the shape of a valley."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/valley"}],"entries":[{"title":"Loire Valley","blocks":[{"text":"The Loire Valley, spanning 280 kilometres (170 mi), is a valley located in the middle stretch of the Loire river in central France, in both the administrative regions Pays de la Loire and Centre-Val de Loire. The area of the Loire Valley comprises about 800 square kilometres (310 sq mi). It is referred to as the Cradle of the French and the Garden of France due to the abundance of vineyards, fruit orchards, and artichoke and asparagus fields, which line the banks of the river. Notable for its historic towns, architecture, and wines, the valley has been inhabited since the Middle Palaeolithic period. In 2000, UNESCO added the central part of the Loire River valley to its list of World Heritage Sites."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Azay_le_rideau.jpg/330px-Azay_le_rideau.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loire_Valley","wikidata":"Q1050483"},{"title":"Loire Valley (wine)","blocks":[{"text":"The Loire Valley wine region includes the French wine regions situated along the river Loire from the Muscadet region near the city of Nantes on the Atlantic coast to the region of Sancerre and Pouilly-Fumé just southeast of the city of Orléans in north central France. In between are the regions of Anjou wine, Saumur, Bourgueil, Chinon, and Vouvray. The Loire Valley itself follows the river through the Loire department to the river's origins in the Cévennes but most of the wine production takes place in the regions noted above. The area includes 87 appellations under the Appellation d'origine contrôlée (AOC) and Indication Géographique Protégée (IGP) systems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Vignobles_val_de_loire-fr.svg/330px-Vignobles_val_de_loire-fr.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loire_Valley_(wine)","wikidata":"Q1954580"},{"title":"Loire","blocks":[{"text":"The Loire is the longest river in France and the 171st-longest in the world. With a length of 1,006 kilometres (625 mi), it drains 117,054 km2 (45,195 sq mi), more than a fifth of France's land, while its average discharge is only half that of the Rhône."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/La_Loire_%C3%A0_La_Bohalle_par_Cramos.JPG/330px-La_Loire_%C3%A0_La_Bohalle_par_Cramos.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loire","wikidata":"Q1469"},{"title":"Valley","blocks":[{"text":"A valley is an elongated low area often running between hills or mountains and typically containing a river or stream running from one end to the other. Most valleys are formed by erosion of the land surface by rivers or streams over a very long period. Some valleys are formed through erosion by glacial ice. These glaciers may remain present in valleys in high mountains or polar areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Salta-VallesCalchaquies-P3140151.JPG/330px-Salta-VallesCalchaquies-P3140151.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valley","wikidata":"Q39816"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Most_beautiful_villages_of_the_world_montsoreau_2.jpg/500px-Most_beautiful_villages_of_the_world_montsoreau_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Most_beautiful_villages_of_the_world_montsoreau_2.jpg/1280px-Most_beautiful_villages_of_the_world_montsoreau_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Most_beautiful_villages_of_the_world_montsoreau_2.jpg/500px-Most_beautiful_villages_of_the_world_montsoreau_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sunset on the Loire River from the Château de Montsoreau-Museum of Contemporary Art","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMost_beautiful_villages_of_the_world_montsoreau_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Vineyard_in_the_Loire_Valley.jpg/500px-Vineyard_in_the_Loire_Valley.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Vineyard_in_the_Loire_Valley.jpg/500px-Vineyard_in_the_Loire_Valley.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Vineyards in the Loire Valley","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVineyard_in_the_Loire_Valley.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Chateau_Valencay_20050726.jpg/330px-Chateau_Valencay_20050726.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Chateau_Valencay_20050726.jpg/330px-Chateau_Valencay_20050726.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Château de Valençay","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChateau_Valencay_20050726.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/France_Loir-et-Cher_Chambord_Chateau_03.jpg/500px-France_Loir-et-Cher_Chambord_Chateau_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimed